# Review rules — {{ react_app_name }}

## Accessibility

Apply [the repo's accessibility skill](../../.agents/skills/accessibility/SKILL.md) as written. It is
WCAG 2.2 AA, and it carries its own evidence contract, severity scale and best-practice-note bucket —
follow those, not a general impression of what an accessibility review is. Consult the bundle that
matches what the diff changed:

- [structure & keyboard](../../.agents/skills/accessibility/references/structure-keyboard.md) —
  interactive elements, `div`/`span` as controls, tabindex, focus, custom widgets, headings, landmarks,
  the app shell, motion
- [names & forms](../../.agents/skills/accessibility/references/names-forms.md) — accessible names,
  `aria-*`, roles, icon-only buttons, `alt`, links, inputs, labels, errors
- [feedback & live regions](../../.agents/skills/accessibility/references/feedback-live.md) — anything
  that changes on screen without a navigation
- [tables & grids](../../.agents/skills/accessibility/references/tables-grids.md) — `<table>`,
  `role="grid"`, sortable/selectable data, tabular data built from `div`s
- [contrast & colour](../../.agents/skills/accessibility/references/contrast-advisory.md) — colour-only
  meaning, selected/active states, focus-ring contrast, theming, disabled styling
- [review scope](../../.agents/skills/accessibility/references/review-scope.md) — the visibility and
  instance rules; applies to every review

Those links load as rules in their own right — verified with `bugbot run verbose=true`, which lists all
seven as included and untruncated. If that ever stops being true and you cannot read them, say so in one
line rather than reviewing from memory of WCAG.

### This repo

- Review the UI anywhere under `{{ react_app_name }}/src/**` — not just `components/`, since UI also
  lives in `App.tsx`, `main.tsx` and `theme/theme-provider.tsx`. `{{ react_app_name }}/index.html` is the
  app shell (`<html lang>`, viewport meta, skip links). The stylesheets are `src/index.css` and
  `src/theme/preset.css`, but colour and contrast work mostly lands in TypeScript and JSON instead:
  `src/theme/colors.json`, `css-variables.ts`, `components-classes.ts` and `tailwind.config.ts`. A change
  to focus, outline, visibility or colour is UI work wherever it lives. Skip
  `{{ react_app_name }}/tests/**` and co-located `**/*.{test,spec}.{ts,tsx}`: those fixtures deliberately
  attach handlers to bare elements, which is why `eslint.config.js` relaxes the interaction rules for
  exactly those globs.

- **`src/components/ui/**` is vendored, not authored here.** It is where shadcn (`new-york`) and
  `@dr-ui` registry components land, per `components.json` — so a fix made there does not flow back
  anywhere, and when a diff touches one, review it as source. Do not reach outside the diff.

- **Wrapped primitives.** Where a component forwards props to a third-party primitive, that library
  supplies roles, `aria-expanded`/`-selected`/`-checked`, keyboard behaviour and focus management — don't
  report their absence on the forwarded element. This **overrides** the skill's "never assume a library
  is accessible": review cannot read `node_modules`, so the behaviour isn't verifiable from the diff and
  asserting it missing is a false positive. Which libraries qualify is repo-specific — see the
  calibration section below, and note that an *unlisted* library is not licence to defer. Three limits:
  - It defers on the **element**, never the file. A hand-rolled control, a local handler, an overridden
    role, or changed structure in the same file still gets the full review.
  - `@radix-ui/react-slot` supplies **no** accessibility behaviour — only polymorphic `asChild`. Watch
    for the shape `const Comp = asChild ? Slot : 'button'` (`ui/button.tsx`): when a handler lands on a
    non-focusable element that way, it is a real 2.1.1 finding, not a deferral. The fallback element
    varies by component, so judge the element, not the pattern. A clean pass-through wrapper that only
    forwards props is still not a finding.
  - Still the wrapper's own job: the accessible **name** (icon-only triggers, a dialog or sheet title),
    the **focus indicator** (these libraries ship no styles), and any DOM node inserted between a Root
    and its typed children.

- **Focus resets.** Tailwind v4 here, so focus styling is utility classes rather than stylesheets —
  judge the rendered outcome, not the class name. The scaffold's own pattern is `outline-none` paired
  with `focus-visible:ring-*` (see `ui/button.tsx`); `outline-hidden` behaves the same way for this
  purpose. Removing the outline on a focus-trapped panel whose focus a primitive manages is conventional
  and not a finding; on a trigger, input, item or row, a reset with no replacement indicator is a real
  finding.

- **Both themes are reachable.** `theme/theme-provider.tsx` toggles the `dark` class on the document
  element, defaulting to the user's `prefers-color-scheme`, so never dismiss a light-mode colour
  problem as "dark theme only" — both themes are in scope. Keep the skill's split intact, though:
  colour-*only* meaning (1.4.1) is a real finding, while contrast *ratio* stays `[advisory — verify]`
  and is therefore dropped under Output. Being in scope does not promote a ratio concern to reportable.

- **`jsx-a11y` recommended already runs on this directory.** `npm run lint` runs in CI on every PR
  touching `{{ react_app_name }}/**`, so anything that plugin catches is already reported there — spend
  the comment budget on what a linter cannot see: whether a name is *meaningful*, whether focus actually
  lands somewhere, whether an async change is announced, whether ARIA matches real behaviour.

- **No automated a11y gate exists** — no axe, jest-axe or vitest-axe in the harness. Don't ask a diff
  to add one, and don't credit it for an assertion you cannot see run.

### Repo-specific calibration

This file ships from the `af-component-react` template as a starting point, and **it is yours to edit**.
Copier keeps your edits as local modifications across updates. The rules above describe only what the
template itself ships — add what your app actually does, because the gaps below cause false positives
rather than missed findings:

- **Primitive libraries in use** (e.g. `@radix-ui/react-*`, `cmdk`, `sonner`, `react-dropzone`) — the
  deferral above needs this list. Without it, review reports missing roles on components that have them.
- **i18n convention** — how accessible names reach translation, which import path, and whether your
  lint setup checks attributes as well as visible text.
- **Any `eslint-disable jsx-a11y/*`** already in `src/**`, and why — so review doesn't re-report it.
- **Forms** — whether a form library owns labelling and error announcement, or the DOM is hand-rolled.
- **Rendered markdown or other generated DOM**, where naming is the app's job rather than the library's.

### Output

- A and AA failures only. Never emit the skill's best-practice notes, at any severity, and drop its
  `[advisory — verify]` findings rather than posting them unlabelled.
- Correctness first — never displace a functional bug.
- Severity — the skill's harm scale mapped onto Cursor's, which the skill itself does not use:
  *Blocks a task* → High, *Serious barrier* → Medium, *Nuisance* → Low.
- At most 3 comments, High exempt. If the cap bites, add one line: "N further A/AA findings not
  itemised".
- One comment per problem. Never open with "consider".
- No clean bill of health. **Overrides** the skill's "say so plainly" — when there is nothing to
  report, report nothing. Its "do not invent issues to look thorough" still holds.
